{
  "_about": "Shared cases for THE TIME STOP's day-29 boundary (NEXT100 #3). One table, three readers: scanner/time_stop.py time_stop_due (the Python definition) and scanner/broker/vivek_run.run_market (the engine that actually closes the row) in tests/test_time_stop_cases.py, and public/js/journal.js timeStopOf (the journal's countdown) in test/time_stop_cases.test.js. Each case is one open long: market, entry_date, tp1_hit and the max_hold (VIVEK_BOT_MAX_HOLD_DAYS) it runs under, with `due` = the expected first market date the time stop may fire (null = no time stop). Each run is one run_market call at `at` (an ISO instant whose offset IS the market's own clock at that instant), with `session` = market_open() at that instant, `held` = market days since entry and `closes` = whether that run must close the row with exit_reason \"time\". The rule the table pins: a pre-TP1 row closes at the first IN-SESSION run held MORE than max_hold days -- day 29 under 28 -- and a stock whose day falls on a weekend goes at Monday's run.",
